### Why is a single limiter insufficient?

A single limiter at any scope has blind spots that other scopes cover.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class SingleLimiterBlindSpots {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single GLOBAL limiter blind spots:");
        System.out.println("  Cannot tell one abusive user from 1000 legitimate ones");
        System.out.println("  Throttling everyone to stop one abuser punishes good users");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Single PER-USER limiter blind spots:");
        System.out.println("  Attacker spreads across 10,000 accounts -> each under per-user limit");
        System.out.println("  Aggregate is massive but per-user looks normal");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Single PER-IP limiter blind spots:");
        System.out.println("  Distributed attack from 10,000 IPs -> each IP under limit");
        System.out.println("  Corporate NAT: 500 legitimate users share one IP -> all throttled");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Solution: COMPOSE all levels so each covers the other's blind spot");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Single GLOBAL limiter: blind to who is causing the load
Single PER-USER limiter: blind to distributed attack across accounts
Single PER-IP limiter: blind to distributed attack + hurts shared IPs

Solution: COMPOSE all levels
```

### How do the levels work together?

A request must pass ALL applicable levels. Whichever pattern is present, the matching level catches it.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class MultiLevelRateLimiting {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Multi-level rate limiting:");
        System.out.println("  Level 1 - Per-user: max 5 OTPs/min per account");
        System.out.println("  Level 2 - Per-IP: max 20 requests/min per IP");
        System.out.println("  Level 3 - Per-provider: max 100 SMS/sec to Twilio");
        System.out.println("  Level 4 - Global: max 10,000 requests/sec total");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Request must pass ALL levels to proceed.");
        System.out.println("  Greedy user? Level 1 catches it.");
        System.out.println("  Script from one IP? Level 2 catches it.");
        System.out.println("  Provider saturated? Level 3 catches it.");
        System.out.println("  Massive flood? Level 4 catches it.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Multi-level rate limiting:
  Level 1 - Per-user: max 5 OTPs/min per account
  Level 2 - Per-IP: max 20 requests/min per IP
  Level 3 - Per-provider: max 100 SMS/sec to Twilio
  Level 4 - Global: max 10,000 requests/sec total

  Request must pass ALL levels to proceed.
```

### What is a token bucket and why per provider?

A token bucket refills at a fixed rate. Each request consumes a token. Empty = denied. Per-provider tracking enables informed failover.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class TokenBucketPerProvider {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Token bucket per provider:");
        System.out.println("  Twilio bucket: capacity=200, refill=100/sec");
        System.out.println("  MSG91 bucket:  capacity=100, refill=50/sec");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Twilio bucket has 0 tokens -> EMPTY");
        System.out.println("  MSG91 bucket has 45 tokens -> AVAILABLE");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Router asks each bucket: 'do you have a token?'");
        System.out.println("  Twilio: NO -> skip");
        System.out.println("  MSG91: YES -> route here");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Clean failover: informed, not blind");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Token bucket per provider:
  Twilio bucket: 0 tokens -> EMPTY
  MSG91 bucket: 45 tokens -> AVAILABLE
  Router routes to MSG91 -> clean failover
```

### What happens without per-provider tracking?

You hammer the exhausted provider, waste threads, and may get penalized.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class HammeringExhaustedProvider {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WITHOUT per-provider bucket:");
        System.out.println("  Twilio rate limit exhausted");
        System.out.println("  System keeps sending to Twilio");
        System.out.println("  Every call rejected with 429");
        System.out.println("  Threads wasted on rejected calls");
        System.out.println("  Twilio may PENALIZE or BLOCK your account");
        System.out.println("  Feedback loop: hammering worsens the situation");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WITH per-provider bucket:");
        System.out.println("  Twilio bucket empty -> do not even try");
        System.out.println("  Route to MSG91 (still has tokens)");
        System.out.println("  Zero wasted calls to Twilio");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WITHOUT per-provider bucket:
  Twilio exhausted -> keep sending -> all rejected -> waste + penalty

WITH per-provider bucket:
  Twilio empty -> skip -> route to MSG91 -> clean delivery
```

### How do you implement multi-level in Spring?

Use Redis counters for per-user and per-IP, token buckets for per-provider, and a global counter at the gateway.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.time.Duration;

@Service
public class MultiLevelLimiter {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public MultiLevelLimiter(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public boolean isAllowed(String userId, String ip) {
        // Level 1: Per-user
        Long userCount = redis.opsForValue().increment("limit:user:" + userId);
        if (userCount == 1) redis.expire("limit:user:" + userId, Duration.ofMinutes(1));
        if (userCount > 5) return false;

        // Level 2: Per-IP
        Long ipCount = redis.opsForValue().increment("limit:ip:" + ip);
        if (ipCount == 1) redis.expire("limit:ip:" + ip, Duration.ofMinutes(1));
        if (ipCount > 20) return false;

        // Level 3 & 4: Per-provider and global handled at gateway
        return true;
    }
}
```

```text
Multi-level check:
  Per-user: userId=rajesh -> count=3 -> PASS
  Per-IP: ip=203.0.113.1 -> count=15 -> PASS
  Per-provider: twilio bucket -> 50 tokens -> PASS
  Global: total=8000/10000 -> PASS
  All levels passed -> request proceeds
```

### How do the levels compare?

| Level | Catches | Blind To |
|---|---|---|
| Per-user | One account monopolizing | Distributed attack across accounts |
| Per-IP | One source hitting many accounts | Distributed attack from many IPs |
| Per-provider | Exceeding downstream's rate | User-level abuse |
| Global | Any flood beyond total capacity | Which user or IP is causing it |

```text
Composed together: each level's blind spot is another level's strength.
Removing any one opens a specific hole.
```

### What is the relationship with circuit breakers?

Token bucket handles the normal rate limit. Circuit breaker handles a provider that is erroring or slow. They complement each other.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class BucketPlusBreaker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Token bucket: tracks remaining allowance (rate limit)");
        System.out.println("Circuit breaker: tracks error rate (health)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Scenario 1: Twilio is healthy but rate-limited");
        System.out.println("  Bucket empty -> fail over to MSG91");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Scenario 2: Twilio is returning 500 errors");
        System.out.println("  Circuit breaker opens -> fail over to MSG91");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Both together: skip provider if EMPTY or UNHEALTHY");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Token bucket: rate limit tracking
Circuit breaker: health tracking
Both together: skip if EMPTY or UNHEALTHY
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Size each bucket to real contracts. Monitor depletion as early warning. Implement in shared Redis. Prefer escalating responses.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Multi-level: per-user + per-IP + per-provider + global
  [x] Token bucket per provider: tracks allowance independently
  [x] Failover: route to provider with tokens when one empties
  [x] Circuit breaker: complement bucket for health-based failover
  [x] Redis: shared counters across all instances
  [x] Monitor: bucket depletion as early-warning metric
  [x] Escalate: CAPTCHA for per-IP, queue for global, 429 for per-user
```
